'''Lake Te Anau''' ({{langx|mi|Te Ana-au}})<ref>{{Cite web |last=Esler |first=Lloyd |date=2022-11-11 |title=Te Anau - a name with a misty history |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/southland-times/news/features/130419024/te-anau--a-name-with-a-misty-history |access-date=2023-12-18 |website=Stuff |language=en}}</ref> is in the southwestern corner of the [[South Island]] of New Zealand. The lake covers an area of {{convert|344|km2|abbr=on}}, making it the [[List of lakes in New Zealand#Largest lakes|second-largest lake]] by surface area in New Zealand (after [[Lake Taupō]]) and the largest in the South Island. It is the second largest lake in Australasia by fresh water volume.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.realjourneys.co.nz/content/library/Background_Info__Caves_Education_Pack.pdf |title=Archived copy |accessdate=2009-08-30 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20100523084313/http://www.realjourneys.co.nz/content/library/Background_Info__Caves_Education_Pack.pdf |archivedate=2010-05-23 }}</ref>{{Better source needed|date=March 2026}}
The main body of the lake runs north-south, and is 65&nbsp;km in length. Three large [[fiord]]s form arms to the lake on its western flank: North Fiord, Middle Fiord and South Fiord. These are the only inland fiords that New Zealand has, the other 14 are out on the coast.{{Citation needed|date=March 2026}} Several small islands lie in the entrance to Middle Fiord, which forks partway along its length into northwest and southwest arms. The surface of the lake is at an altitude of 210 m. It has a maximum depth of 425 m,<ref>{{cite web |url = http://www.mfe.govt.nz/publications/ser/snapshot-lake-water-quality-nov06/html/page4.html |title = Lakes in New Zealand |publisher = [[Ministry for the Environment (New Zealand)|Ministry for the Environment]] |url-status = dead |archiveurl = https://web.archive.org/web/20081015213629/http://www.mfe.govt.nz/publications/ser/snapshot-lake-water-quality-nov06/html/page4.html |archivedate = 2008-10-15 }}</ref> so much of its bed lies below sea level, with the deepest part of the lake being 215 metres below sea level.
[[File:Te Anau control gates.jpg|thumb|The Lake Te Anau control gates which control the flow of water from Lake Te Anau into the Waiau river and maintain the water level between 201.5 and 202.7 metres above sea level.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lake levels |url=https://www.meridianenergy.co.nz/power-stations/lake-levels |access-date=2023-05-05 |website=Meridian Energy |language=en-US}}</ref>]]
Several rivers feed the lake, of which the most important is the [[Eglinton River]], which joins the lake from the east, opposite the entrance to North Fiord. The outflow is the [[Waiau River, Southland|Waiau River]], which flows south for several kilometres into [[Lake Manapouri]]. The town of [[Te Anau]] lies at the south-eastern corner of the lake, close to the outflow.

Most of the lake is within [[Fiordland National Park]] and the [[Te Wahipounamu]] [[World Heritage Site]], the latter of which was officially recognised internationally in 1990. Other than the Te Anau township, the only human habitation close to the lake is the farming settlement of Te Anau Downs, close to the mouth of the Eglinton River. Between these two settlements the land is rolling hill country, but elsewhere the land is mountainous, especially along its western shore, where the [[Kepler Mountains|Kepler]] and [[Murchison Mountains]] rise 1,400 m above the surface of the lake.{{cn|date=September 2024}}

Lake Te Anau is important for the Ngāi Tahu iwi,{{efn|The Māori word for tribe}} in pre-European times, prior to being displaced by European encroachment, the area was a traditional stopping point on their trails between the east and west coasts of the South Island of New Zealand, where they obtained food and resources. The lake was first discovered by Europeans in 1852, by explorers Charles Nairn and William Stephen.

Two [[New Zealand Great Walks]] start at the lake. The [[Milford Track]] starts at the northern tip of the lake and the [[Kepler Track]] starts and ends at the south tip of the lake at the Waiau River.{{Citation needed|date=March 2026}}

==Name==
The English translation of the [[Māori language|Māori]] name Te Anau has been disputed. It was supposed by many that Te Anau was the name of the granddaughter of Hekeia, a chief of the [[Waitaha (South Island iwi)|Waitaha]] tribe, whose name now belongs to a mountain on the [[Longwood Range]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=McLintock|first1=A. H. |authorlink1=An Encyclopaedia of New Zealand|title=Te Anau, Lake |url= http://www.TeAra.govt.nz/en/1966/te-anau-lake |website=Te Ara – The Encyclopedia of New Zealand |accessdate=6 September 2015 |date=1966}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Taylor|first1=W. A. |title=Lore and History of the South Island Maori|date=1952 |publisher=Bascands |page=148 |url= http://nzetc.victoria.ac.nz/tm/scholarly/tei-TayLore-t1-body1-d17.html |accessdate=6 September 2015 |chapter=Murihiki}}</ref> When the [[Te Ana-au Caves]] were rediscovered in 1948, earlier explanations of the name were replaced by the interpretation of the full name Te Ana-au, Māori for 'The cave of swirling water'.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://nzhistory.govt.nz/keyword/te-anau |title=Te Anau |work=NZHistory |publisher= Ministry of Culture and Heritage |access-date=25 September 2015}}</ref>

==Flora and fauna==
Numerous species of wildlife and vegetation are found in the watershed of Lake Te Anau. Vegetative understory includes numerous fern species including the crown fern, ''[[Blechnum discolor]]''.<ref>C. Michael Hogan. 2009</ref>

Several species of [[endangered]] birds live around the shores of Lake Te Anau, notably the [[takahē]] (''Notornis hochstetteri''). An area between the Middle and South Fiords called the Murchison Mountains is a sanctuary set aside for these birds. The western shore of the lake also features the [[Te Ana-au Caves]].

==Recreation==
Throughout summer the lake is used for boating, fishing, kayaking, swimming and access to [[Fiordland National Park]]. The lake is home to both the Marakura Yacht Club and the Te Anau Boating Club.<ref>{{cite web |url= https://teanauboatclub.org.nz/ |title=Te Anau Boating Club |accessdate=15 February 2017}}</ref> Every year on the weekend of [[Labour Day]] a fishing competition is held.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.teanauevents.co.nz/event/te-anau-manapouri-fishing-classic |title=Te Anau Manapouri Fishing Classic |accessdate=15 February 2017}}</ref>

